–352 (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=60821d7430db63e3677ed0fc23dba560afc97973&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; The minimum period of the Ehrhart ... If P ⊂Rd is a rational polytope, then iP (n) := #(nP ∩Zd) is a quasi-polynomial in n, called ... McAllister), kwoods@math.berkeley.edu (K.M.\nJournal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A 109 (2005) 345–352\nwww.elsevier.com/locate/jcta\nNote\nThe minimum period of the Ehrhart\nquasi-polynomial of a rational polytope\nTyrrell B. McAllistera,1, Kevin M. Woodsb,2\naDepartment of Mathematics, University of California, One Shields Avenue, Davis, CA 95616, USA\nbUniversity of California, Berkeley, USA\nReceived 23 January 2004\nAbstract\nIf P ⊂Rd is a rational polytope, then iP (n) := #(nP ∩Zd) is a quasi-polynomial in n, called\nthe Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of P . The minimum period of iP (n) must divide D(P ) = min{n ∈\nZ>0: nP is an integral polytope}. Few examples are known where the minimum period is not exactly\nD(P ). We show that for any D, there is a 2-dimensional triangle P such that D(P ) = D but such\nthat the minimum period of iP (n) is 1, that is, iP (n) is a polynomial in n. We also characterize all\npolygons P such that iP (n) is a polynomial. In addition, we provide a counterexample to a conjecture\nby T. Zaslavsky about the periods of the coefﬁcients of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial.\n© 2004 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nKeywords: Ehrhart polynomials; Hilbert series; Lattice points; Convex bodies\n1. Introduction\nAn integral (respectively, rational) polytope is a polytope whose vertices have integral\n(respectively, rational) coordinates. Given a rational polytope P ⊂Rd, the denominator\nof P is\nD(P ) = min{n ∈Z>0: nP is an integral polytope}.\nE-mail address: tmcal@math.ucdavis.edu (T.B. McAllister), kwoods@math.berkeley.edu (K.M. Woods).\n1 Partially supported by NSF VIGRE Grant No. DMS-0135345.\n2 Partially supported by an NSF Graduate Research Fellowship.\n0097-3165/$ - see front matter © 2004 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\ndoi:10.1016/j.jcta.2004.08.006--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputing the period of an Ehrhart (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=db09550b3676709b731560e47f538c6db15c32cd&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; quasi-polynomial in t, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of P. ... a rational polytope P ⊂Rd and an integer n, decides whether n is a ... there is a polynomial time algorithm to compute the minimum period\nComputing the period of an Ehrhart\nquasi-polynomial\nKevin M. Woods∗\nFebruary 1, 2008\nAbstract\nIf P ⊂Rd is a rational polytope, then iP (t) := #(tP ∩Zd) is a\nquasi-polynomial in t, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of P. A\nperiod of iP (t) is D(P), the smallest D ∈Z+ such that D · P has\nintegral vertices. Often, D(P) is the minimum period of iP (t), but, in\nseveral interesting examples, the minimum period is smaller. We prove\nthat, for ﬁxed d, there is a polynomial time algorithm which, given\na rational polytope P ⊂Rd and an integer n, decides whether n is a\nperiod of iP (t). In particular, there is a polynomial time algorithm to\ndecide whether iP (t) is a polynomial. We conjecture that, for ﬁxed d,\nthere is a polynomial time algorithm to compute the minimum period\nof iP (t). The tools we use are rational generating functions.\n1\nIntroduction\narXiv:math/0411207v1  [math.CO]  9 Nov 2004\nGiven a rational polytope P ⊂Rd (that is, a bounded subset of Rd which is\ndeﬁned by a ﬁnite collection of integer linear inequalities), deﬁne the function\niP(t) = #(tP ∩Zd),\nwhere tP is P dilated by a factor of t. Also deﬁne D = D(P) to be the\nsmallest D ∈Z+ such that D · P has integral vertices.\nEhrhart proved\n[Ehr62] that iP(t) is a quasi-polynomial function with a period of D.\nIn\n∗Partially supported by a Clay LiftoﬀFellowship and NSF Grant DMS 0402148.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope (https://escholarship.org/uc/item/0z92d34k)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; The Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope ... The Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope ...   * McAllister, Tyrrell B.\nThe Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope\n\n\nThis item is not available for download from eScholarship\n\n## \n\nThe Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope\n\n2003\n\n  * McAllister, Tyrrell B.;\n  * Woods, Kevin M.\n\n## Published Web Location\n\nhttps://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0310255.pdf\n\nNo data is associated with this publication.\n\n[Button: ...][Button: Main Content][Button: Metrics]Author & Article Info\n\nAbstract\n\nIf $P\\subset \\R^d$ is a rational polytope, then $i_P(n):=#(nP\\cap \\Z^d)$ is a quasi-polynomial in $n$, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of $P$. The period of $i_P(n)$ must divide $\\LL(P)= \\min \\{n \\in \\Z_{> 0} \\colon nP \\text{is an integral polytope}\\}$. Few examples are known where the period is not exactly $\\LL(P)$. We show that for any $\\LL$, there is a 2-dimensional triangle $P$ such that $\\LL(P)=\\LL$ but such that the period of $i_P(n)$ is 1, that is, $i_P(n)$ is a polynomial in $n$. We also characterize all polygons $P$ such that $i_P(n)$ is a polynomial. In addition, we provide a counterexample to a conjecture by T. Zaslavsky about the periods of the coefficients of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0310255)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.1 years ago; Title: The Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope ... McAllister, Kevin M. ... If $P\\subset \\R^d$ is a rational polytope, then $i_P(n):=#(nP\\cap \\Z^d)$ is a quasi-polynomial in $n$, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of $P$.\nTitle: The Minimum Period of the Ehrhart Quasi-polynomial of a Rational Polytope\nAuthors: Tyrrell B. McAllister, Kevin M. Woods\nDate: Thu Oct 16 22:52:15 2003\n\nIf $P\\subset \\R^d$ is a rational polytope, then $i_P(n):=#(nP\\cap \\Z^d)$ is a quasi-polynomial in $n$, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of $P$. The period of $i_P(n)$ must divide $\\LL(P)= \\min \\{n \\in \\Z_{> 0} \\colon nP \\text{is an integral polytope}\\}$. Few examples are known where the period is not exactly $\\LL(P)$. We show that for any $\\LL$, there is a 2-dimensional triangle $P$ such that $\\LL(P)=\\LL$ but such that the period of $i_P(n)$ is 1, that is, $i_P(n)$ is a polynomial in $n$. We also characterize all polygons $P$ such that $i_P(n)$ is a polynomial. In addition, we provide a counterexample to a conjecture by T. Zaslavsky about the periods of the coefficients of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputing the Period of an Ehrhart Quasi-Polynomial (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v12i1r34)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.3 years ago; Crawled: last month;   * Kevin Woods ... If $P\\subset {\\Bbb R}^d$ is a rational polytope, then $i_P(t):=\\#(tP\\cap {\\Bbb Z}^d)$ is a quasi-polynomial in $t$, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of $P$. ... We conjecture that, for fixed $d$, there is a polynomial time algorithm to compute the minimum period of $i_P(t)$.\n\n# Computing the Period of an Ehrhart Quasi-Polynomial\n\n  * Kevin Woods\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/1931\n\n### Abstract\n\nIf $P\\subset {\\Bbb R}^d$ is a rational polytope, then $i_P(t):=\\#(tP\\cap {\\Bbb Z}^d)$ is a quasi-polynomial in $t$, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of $P$. A period of $i_P(t)$ is ${\\cal D}(P)$, the smallest ${\\cal D}\\in {\\Bbb Z}_+$ such that ${\\cal D}\\cdot P$ has integral vertices. Often, ${\\cal D}(P)$ is the minimum period of $i_P(t)$, but, in several interesting examples, the minimum period is smaller. We prove that, for fixed $d$, there is a polynomial time algorithm which, given a rational polytope $P\\subset{\\Bbb R}^d$ and an integer $n$, decides whether $n$ is a period of $i_P(t)$. In particular, there is a polynomial time algorithm to decide whether $i_P(t)$ is a polynomial. We conjecture that, for fixed $d$, there is a polynomial time algorithm to compute the minimum period of $i_P(t)$. The tools we use are rational generating functions.\n\nPublished\n\n2005-07-29\n\nHow to Cite\n\nWoods, K. (2005). Computing the Period of an Ehrhart Quasi-Polynomial. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 12(1), #R34. https://doi.org/10.37236/1931\n\nIssue\n\nVolume 12 (2005)\n\nArticle Number\n\nR34--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApplications of Polyhedral Geometry to Computational Representation (https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~reiner/REU/REU2016notes/McAllisterThesis.pdf)\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; The minimum quasi-period of the Ehrhart\nquasi-polynomial of a rational polytope ... however: rational 1-dimensional polytopes always have full quasi-period (Theorem\nAPPENDIX A\nThe minimum quasi-period of the Ehrhart\nquasi-polynomial of a rational polytope\nA.1. Introduction\nRecall that an integral (respectively, rational) polytope is a polytope whose\nvertices have integral (respectively, rational) coordinates. Given a rational polytope\nP ⊂Rd, the denominator of P is\nD(P) = min{n ∈Z>0 : nP is an integral polytope}.\nEhrhart proved [19] that, if P ⊂Rd is a rational polytope, then there is a\nquasi-polynomial function iP : Z 7→Z with quasi-period D(P) such that, for n ≥0,\niP (n) = |nP ∩Zd|.\nThis means that there exist polynomial functions f1, . . . , fD(P ) such that iP (n) =\nfj(n) for n ≡j mod D(P). In particular, if P is integral, then D(P) = 1, so iP is\na polynomial function. We call iP the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of P.\nWe know that D(P) is a quasi-period of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of P,\nbut what is the minimum quasi-period? Of course, it must divide D(P), and it very\noften equals D(P). Though this is not always the case, very few counterexamples\nwere previously known. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nv12i1r34.dvi (https://www2.oberlin.edu/faculty/kwoods/research/qpperiod.pdf)\nciteturn0search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Computing the period of an Ehrhart quasi-polynomial ... If P ⊂Rd is a rational polytope, then iP (t) := #(tP ∩Zd) is a quasi-polynomial ... period of iP (t), but, in several interesting examples, the minimum period is smaller.\nComputing the period of an Ehrhart quasi-polynomial\nKevin Woods∗\nDepartment of Mathematics\nUniversity of California, Berkeley, USA\nkwoods@math.berkeley.edu\nSubmitted: Jun 9, 2005; Accepted: Jun 24, 2005; Published: Jul 29, 2005\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcations: 05A15, 68W30, 52C07\nAbstract\nIf P ⊂Rd is a rational polytope, then iP (t) := #(tP ∩Zd) is a quasi-polynomial\nin t, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of P.\nA period of iP (t) is D(P), the\nsmallest D ∈Z+ such that D · P has integral vertices. Often, D(P) is the minimum\nperiod of iP (t), but, in several interesting examples, the minimum period is smaller.\nWe prove that, for ﬁxed d, there is a polynomial time algorithm which, given a\nrational polytope P ⊂Rd and an integer n, decides whether n is a period of iP (t).\nIn particular, there is a polynomial time algorithm to decide whether iP (t) is a\npolynomial. We conjecture that, for ﬁxed d, there is a polynomial time algorithm\nto compute the minimum period of iP (t). The tools we use are rational generating\nfunctions.\n1\nIntroduction\nGiven a rational polytope P ⊂Rd (that is, a bounded subset of Rd which is deﬁned by a\nﬁnite collection of integer linear inequalities), deﬁne the function\niP(t) = #(tP ∩Zd),\nwhere tP is P dilated by a factor of t. Also deﬁne D = D(P) to be the smallest D ∈Z+\nsuch that D · P has integral vertices.\nEhrhart proved [Ehr62] that iP(t) is a quasi-\npolynomial function with a period of D. In other words, there exist polynomial functions\nf0(t), f1(t), . . . , fD−1(t), called the constituents of iP(t), such that\niP(t) = fj(t) for t ≡j (mod D).\nExample 1. P = [0, 1\n2] × [0, 1\n2] ⊂R2.\n∗Partially supported by a Clay LiftoﬀFellowship and NSF Grant DMS 0402148.\nthe electronic journal of combinatorics 12 (2005), #R34\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe minimum period of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of a rational polytope (https://research.tue.nl/p/en/research-outputs/the-minimum-period-of-the-ehrhart-quasi-polynomial-of-a-rational-)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; # The minimum period of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of a rational polytope ... McAllister ... The minimum period of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of a rational polytope.\n\n# The minimum period of the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of a rational polytope\n\n  * T.B. McAllister\n\n,\n  * K.M. Woods\n\nOutput:\n\nContribution to journal\n\nArticle\n\nAcademic\n\nPeer-review\n\nInformation\n\n### \n\nOutput type\n\nOutput:\n\nContribution to journal\n\nArticle\n\nAcademic\n\nPeer-review\n\n### \n\nOriginal language\n\nEnglish\n\n### \n\nPages from-to (Number of pages)\n\nPages 345-352\n\n### \n\nJournal (Volume, Issue Number)\n\nJournal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A (Volume 109, Issue 2)\n\n### \n\nPublication milestones\n\n  * Published - 2005\n\n### \n\nPublication status\n\nPublished - 2005\n\n### \n\nISSN\n\n0097-3165\n\nAbstract\n\nIf is a rational polytope, then is a quasi-polynomial in n, called the Ehrhart quasi-polynomial of P. The minimum 